  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and RAP analysis indicate continued \nridging across the Four Corners as a stationary boundary sits across \nnorthern Kansas. Residual showers from a midlevel baroclinic zone \ndrifted over portions of northern Kansas earlier today, and cloud \ncover from these has lingered over central Kansas. We continue to \nexpect hot conditions across most locations through the remainder of \nthe afternoon into the early evening, particularly south of US-50 \nwhere cloud coverage is limited. Looking ahead to Tuesday, little \nchange in the synoptic pattern is expected with the ridge in place. \nA slight northward shift in the surface boundary along with a subtle \nincrease in the westerly (downslope) component of surface winds \nshould allow for hotter temperatures in the afternoon and early \nevening. As such, many locations should see heat indices \naround/above 105 degrees. Similar to today, there are questions as \nto how hot temperatures get across portions of central Kansas given \nhigh clouds overhead heading into the afternoon. However, current \nthinking is that heat indices should still be able to clear advisory \ncriteria areawide, so decided to issue a heat advisory for the \nentire forecast area Tuesday afternoon into the early evening. \nAnother day of heat is expected Wednesday with widespread highs \nforecast above 100 degrees.\n\nGlobal models continue to highlight a change to the overall pattern \nlate Wednesday as midlevel energy arrives across the Pacific \nNorthwest and further south across southern California. These two \nfeatures will allow for southwesterly mid/upper flow to return to \nthe central CONUS through the weekend. Perturbations in this flow \ntrekking out of the Rockies will bring repeated chances for showers \nand storms to portions of the Plains beginning Wednesday night and \nlasting through the weekend, though as this morning's \ndiscussion pointed out, exactly how far south these chances make\nit remains uncertain. Trends in long-term guidance so far \ncontinue to suggest locations to our north and west will have \nthe greatest chances for meaningful precipitation, and with the \nsurface boundary continuing to linger across northern Kansas, \nthis would suggest hot and dry conditions through at least \nThursday. Models hint at the potential for a southward shift of \nthe boundary toward the end of the week, which may bring better \nchances for rain to the forecast area as we head into the \nweekend.",
